@page "/CacheTagsDemo"
@model LaughTale.Showcase.Pages.CacheTagsDemoModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Cache Tags & Incremental Regeneration";
}

<div class="app-container" style="max-width: 900px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2rem; padding-bottom: 3rem;">

    <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--p-surface-0) 0%, var(--p-surface-50) 100%); border: 1px solid var(--p-border-color); border-radius: 1.25rem; padding: 2.5rem 2rem; box-shadow: 0 10px 25px -5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.04);">
        <h1 style="font-size: 1.875rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--p-text-color); margin: 0;">Cache Tags &amp; Incremental Regeneration</h1>
        <p style="color: var(--p-text-muted); font-size: 0.875rem; margin: 0.25rem 0 0;">ROADMAP.v5.md Part F: this page is cached for 15 seconds (a thin wrapper over ASP.NET Core's own <code>OutputCache</code> - the "incremental regeneration" half: it goes stale and regenerates on its own). It's also tagged <code>@LaughTale.Showcase.Pages.CacheTagsDemoModel.Tag</code> at render time via <code>HttpContext.Tag(...)</code>, so the button below can evict it on demand - the "cache tags &amp; live invalidation" half - without waiting for the 15s duration.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="background: var(--p-surface-0); border: 1px solid var(--p-border-color); border-radius: 1rem; padding: 1.75rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem;">
        <div>
            <span class="aura-tag tag-cyan" style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem; display: inline-block;">Rendered at</span>
            <p style="margin: 0; color: var(--p-surface-700); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700;">@Model.RenderedAtUtc.ToString("HH:mm:ss.fff") UTC</p>
            <p style="margin: 0.25rem 0 0; color: var(--p-text-muted); font-size: 0.75rem;">Nonce: @Model.RenderNonce</p>
        </div>
        <p style="color: var(--p-text-muted); font-size: 0.8125rem; margin: 0;">
            Reload this page repeatedly within 15 seconds - the timestamp and nonce stay IDENTICAL (served from cache). Click "Invalidate now" to evict the tag immediately, then reload - the timestamp changes right away, proving eviction forces regeneration without waiting for the duration to expire.
        </p>
        <a href="/CacheTagsDemoInvalidate" class="p-button p-button-secondary" style="display: inline-block; width: fit-content; text-decoration: none;">Invalidate now</a>
    </div>

    <div style="background: var(--p-surface-0); border: 1px solid var(--p-border-color); border-radius: 1rem; padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--p-text-muted);">
        <strong>Real caveat found live-verifying this feature:</strong> the mutation trigger lives on its own separate page (<code>/CacheTagsDemoInvalidate</code>), not a form on THIS page. Rendering an antiforgery token (<code>@@Html.AntiForgeryToken()</code>/<code>&lt;island-form&gt;</code>) makes ASP.NET Core itself set <code>Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store</code> on the response carrying it - confirmed live, not assumed. Putting that form here would have silently made this page uncacheable, for reasons entirely unrelated to <code>RespectNoStorePolicy</code>. Keep a cached page's own response free of antiforgery-token rendering; put mutation triggers on their own uncached surface.
    </div>

</div>
